    "burst_concatenation": {
      "decision": "(a) bursts concatenated in chronological file order, ignoring the real 10s gap between bursts -- series treated as if index-contiguous. Declared simplification: the true calendar-time correlation structure across the 10s gaps is truncated/discarded; correlation structure WITHIN each 0.1s/2560-sample burst remains real. This affects the physical meaning of tau in the concatenated series: tau=k means 'k raw samples', which is 'k*39.06us of real recording time' within a burst, but coarse-graining blocks that straddle a burst boundary silently splice together samples that are actually 10s apart in real time."
    },
    "transition_20g": {
      "criterion": "first sample (in chronological burst-concatenated order) where |horizontal_accel_g| > 20g (Nectoux et al. 2012 end-of-life criterion)",
      "global_sample_index_0based": 7053117,
      "value_g": -20.81599998474121,
      "file_1based": 2756,
      "within_file_index_0based": 317,
      "file_name": "acc_02756.csv"
    },
    "segments": {
      "pre_full_n_samples": 7053117,
      "post_full_n_samples": 122563,
      "pre_decimation_factor": 200,
      "pre_decimation_method": "uniform stride subsampling pre_full[::200] (NOT block-averaging -- distinct from the MSE pipeline's own tau coarse-graining). Declared deviation from METHODOLOGY_NOTE.md, which did not anticipate PRE segments of ~7e6 samples: naive-cost SampEn on the full 7,053,117-sample PRE was benchmarked (via power-law extrapolation from N=20k/50k/100k/200k timings, ~N^1.48 empirical scaling with the KD-tree implementation) at roughly 20 minutes for a SINGLE SampEn(tau=1) call, which would make the N_SURROGATES=200 IAAFT protocol (requiring ~400 such calls for PRE alone) computationally infeasible (~60+ hours). Decimating by 200 keeps the full 200-surrogate IAAFT protocol (mandated as PRIMARY by METHODOLOGY_NOTE.md gap (c)) tractable (~1 hour total).",
      "post_decimation": "NONE -- POST was left at full native (25.6kHz-equivalent, burst-concatenated) resolution for both primary and robustness variants, since it is small enough (122,563 / 61,281 samples) to be computationally tractable directly (benchmarked ~11s per compute_mse call, ~3s per IAAFT surrogate generation) and is the physically most important segment (captures the actual failure transient).",
      "pre_primary_n_samples": 35266,
      "pre_robust_n_samples": 17633,
      "post_primary_n_samples": 122563,
      "post_robust_n_samples": 61281,
      "pre_robust_rule": "most recent 50% by sample count of the DECIMATED pre_primary series (pre_decimated[len//2:])",
      "post_robust_rule": "the 50% of post_full closest to the transition, i.e. post_full[:len//2] (post_full is already ordered starting at the transition sample and moving forward in time)"
    }
